<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pastoral Care</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 590</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Regula Pastoralis, Liber Regulae Pastoralis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pastoral Care (Regula Pastoralis) is a treatise written by Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a> around 590 on the duties and conduct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and clergy. Widely read in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was translated into Old English in the circle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, with a preface in the king's name lamenting the decline of learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and announcing his programme of translations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pastoral-care.jpg" alt="Pastoral Care" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gregory the Great, unknown writer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:01_01B_504_4TroyesMS504PastoralCare.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
